<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Act of Union (1815)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1815</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Riksakten</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Act of Union of 1815, Riksakten, was the law adopted by the Norwegian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> and the Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> that set the terms of the personal union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. It confirmed that the two kingdoms shared a king and conducted foreign policy jointly, while each kept its own constitution, parliament, government and laws. It remained in force until the union was dissolved in 1905.</p></div>
